1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: All matter? Electrons, atoms, everything?

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: In principle, yes.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: And in nineteen twenty-seven, experiments confirmed it for electrons, bouncing them off crystals and through thin films.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: But for decades, the two-slit version with electrons was taught only as a thought experiment.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: This experiment, Richard Feynman even wrote in his famous lectures, has never been done in just this way.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: Except it had, in 1961, the year those lectures began.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: So who did it?

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: Klaus Yernsson, in Tubingen, made slits for electrons and saw the fringes, with up to five slits.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: His paper was in German, and didn't appear in English until nineteen seventy-four.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: That same year, in Bologna, Merli, Missiroli and Pozzi sent electrons through one at a time.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: They didn't use slits, though. They used an electron biprism, which is a fine wire that splits the beam in two.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: Then in 1989, Akira Tonomura and colleagues at Hitachi made it cleaner still.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: Fewer than a thousand electrons arrived each second, so two were never in the apparatus at once.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: So there was nothing for an electron to interfere with, except itself.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: Each one arrived as a single flash. Here are eight electrons, then two hundred and seventy, then two thousand.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: And over a twenty-minute exposure, 60,000 electrons drew the stripes.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: In two thousand and two, Physics World readers voted the single-electron version the most beautiful experiment in physics.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERB: So how big can a thing be and still do this?

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: In nineteen ninety-one, helium atoms interfered after passing through slits in gold foil.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERB: In 1999, a team in Vienna sent buckyballs, molecules of 60 carbon atoms, through a grating of many slits.
